# Impact Analyzer — project outline

## Implementation checkpoint — 7 October 2026

The demo supports numeric parameter changes and textual behaviour changes in a
synthetic coffee-machine model. Its 15 entities and 13 relationships span six
types: parameters, modules, behaviours, requirements, tests, and documentation topics.

The browser validates the model, selects review candidates, and explains each
candidate with a relationship path. Reviewers record statuses and notes and copy
a live report. The static app needs no backend. Python provides a reference CLI
and optional local API; 26 tests check both engines, public assets, and HTTP routes.

The bounded demo completes the ten phases below. Read the
[case study](portfolio-case-study.md) for the outcome and limitations, or the
[development guide](development.md) to run and publish it.

## Problem and goal

**User:** systems engineer, technical writer, or knowledge engineer.

**Situation:** an engineer proposes a change to a product setting or behaviour.
Requirements, software, tests, and documentation each hold part of the design knowledge.

**Goal:** use explicit relationships to identify artifacts that may need review
and explain why the analyzer selects them.

**Core question:** when one thing changes, what else might require review?

## Development phases

| Phase | Work | Deliverable |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1. Define the problem | Identify the user, change scenario, and boundaries | Problem statement |
| 2. Model the system | Choose entity types and review units | Domain model |
| 3. Define relationships | Specify the meaning and direction of each relationship | Relationship model |
| 4. Create synthetic data | Write inspectable examples and independent branches | JSON dataset |
| 5. Build the graph | Connect entities through stored relationships | Graph representation |
| 6. Build review traversal | Follow a bounded policy from the changed entity | Analysis engine |
| 7. Add documentation review | Connect topics to parameters and behaviours | Specific documentation candidates |
| 8. Build the interface | Let reviewers propose changes and record findings | Browser demo |
| 9. Explain candidates | Preserve stored edges and traversal directions | Inspectable paths |
| 10. Write the case study | Explain the problem, approach, validation, and limits | Portfolio narrative |

## Model and traversal decisions

Define relationship meanings before choosing traversal directions. For example,
the graph stores `Behaviour → implementedBy → SoftwareModule`. A parameter
review follows that edge backwards from the module and displays “implements.”

Use documentation topics as review units. Connect them to parameters through
`references` and to behaviours through `describes`. Preserve the stored edges so
reviewers can inspect the evidence behind each result.

Follow only the approved review policy. Stop at tests and documentation topics;
for behaviour changes, also stop at software modules. Track visited entities
and return one shortest explanation path per candidate. Treat each result as a
review suggestion, not proof of an engineering consequence.

## Working scenarios

- [Temperature](first-working-scenario.md): raise the brew target from 93°C to
  95°C; identify four engineering artifacts and two documentation topics.
- [Pressure](second-working-scenario.md): raise the target from 9 to 10 bar;
  identify a separate engineering branch that shares one specification topic.
- [Behaviour](third-working-scenario.md): require five continuous seconds within
  temperature tolerance before brewing; identify a module, requirement, test, and topic.

Use the separate steam setting to verify that traversal excludes unrelated artifacts.

## Broader ideas and scope

The original plan considered features, hardware components, alerts, larger
datasets, and an overview graph. The current demo uses a smaller model and
per-candidate explanation paths. It does not implement those broader ideas.

Prioritize relationship quality over record count. Expand the model only when a
new scenario demonstrates a useful review relationship or boundary. Evaluate a
graph database only if the model or workload needs one.

Keep the finish line concrete: propose a change, identify review candidates,
inspect their paths, and explain which documentation may need review. Physical
simulation, inferred dependencies, persistence, collaboration, and alerts-project
integration remain outside the demo’s scope.
